analysisWe need to produce an assert statement matching the comment: "If the bit index is non-negative, then the value of the bit at that index is toggled from its previous state."

We are inside method flip(int bitIndex). The method flips the bit at that index. The comment says: If bitIndex >=0, then the value at that index after operation is the opposite of its previous value. So we need an assert expression: (bitIndex >= 0) => (this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex))). However, note that the method uses internal words and recalc, but we have observer method get(int). So we can use this.get(bitIndex). Also need to ensure that we refer to pre-state using \old.

Thus assert: bitIndex >= 0 => this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));

We need to output as Java assert statement inside comment? The examples show just the assert line with no semicolon? They include semicolon after assert? In examples they wrote: <code>assert this.contains(value);</code> (no trailing semicolon inside code block). Actually they wrote "assert this.contains(value);" inside code block - includes semicolon. So we should include semicolon.

Thus final answer: <code>assert bitIndex >= 0 => this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));</code>

Check syntax: In JML, you would write "assert (bitIndex >= 0) ==> this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));". But they use "=>" as implication operator per instructions. So we use "=>" not "==>". So final: assert bitIndex >= 0 => this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));

Thus final.assistantfinal<code>assert bitIndex >= 0 => this.get(bitIndex) == !\old(this.get(bitIndex));</code>
